# Ralph for OpenCode

A locally installable OpenCode plugin with durable session jobs, independent code and functional review, bounded review repairs, a read-only dashboard, and interruption recovery. Exact tested dependencies and platform results are in [compatibility](docs/opencode-compatibility.md).

Ralph feature questions and scope authorization use OpenCode’s native question forms. `/ralph-orchestrator` opens a fresh interview with the full grill-me skill; approval injects the full create-prd skill into that session. Accepted PRD completion opens a fresh issue-planning session with the full adapted to-issues skill. Valid issue completion starts execution automatically. Independent workers and reviewers retain their sessions and Git worktrees; progress and results appear in the issue-planning chat. Installed-host and native platform evidence for this stage workflow is tracked separately in [package validation](docs/package-validation.md).

## Build and install locally

Use Git and the Bun version in `.bun-version` (also pinned in `package.json`). From this source directory:

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run pack:local
```

Copy `opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz` to each target repository, then run there:

```sh
bun add ./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz
bun -e 'console.log(require("node:url").pathToFileURL(require("node:fs").realpathSync("node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin")).href)'
```

Add the generated, encoded URL to the repository's `opencode.json`, preserving any existing settings:

```json
{
  "plugins": ["REPLACE_WITH_GENERATED_PACKAGE_DIRECTORY_URL"]
}
```

Use the **entire** URL printed by the command. It points to the package directory, where OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js`; do not point it to `dist/plugin.js`. This handles Windows drives and spaces. The example uses single quotes around JavaScript for PowerShell and POSIX shells; native Windows results are pending CI. A relative tarball dependency is recommended; Bun 1.4.3 failed to install the tested percent-encoded absolute `file:` tarball URL with spaces. Each target must be a Git repository. The repository root need not have a commit just to load the plugin; feature/review operations need committed inputs.

Keep standalone Bun on PATH: compiled OpenCode cannot execute the owned wrapper scripts through its own executable.

Start OpenCode **2.0.29** in that repository (see compatibility for the local binary path). Restart OpenCode after changing plugin configuration. No registry publication is required or performed by these scripts.

## Commands and operation

- `/ralph-commands` asks the current assistant to search the project and show the proposed commands. Reply with your approval or changes in the same chat. After approval, `ralph_save_commands` validates and saves `.ralph/commands.json`, then finishes. Setup does not execute the commands; execution runs its own baseline checks.

- `/ralph-orchestrator [feature]` directly opens and focuses a fresh interview. Its native opening question always asks what to build, showing supplied text for confirmation. The interviewer explores the repository and asks recommended native questions. `ralph_complete_interview` presents an ASD-STE100-guided explanation, scope, criteria, branch and exact HEAD. One native approval authorizes PRD creation, issue planning and implementation within that scope. `ralph_write_planning_file` confines authoring to run artifacts; `ralph_complete_prd` validates the PRD and opens the fresh planner; `ralph_complete_issues` validates the graph/files and starts baseline and execution without another approval. Prose cannot advance stages.

- `/ralph-status [run-id]` reads durable status and the dashboard URL. With no argument it selects the latest run.
- `/ralph-pause [run-id]` records pause intent and freezes new dispatch while active work settles.
- `/ralph-resume [run-id]` resumes supported saved planning, baseline, execution or review after explicit recovery checks. Before PRD acceptance it offers fresh discovery or retention; accepted stages retain artifacts, owners and remaining attempts. Unknown ownership or changed artifacts block resume. `--base <commit>` requests renewed explanation approval for the checked-out starting commit; `--review` retries an eligible unverified report within its remaining review budget. Changed HEAD or commands require renewed explanation approval. Unsupported saved protocols require a fresh workflow; they never execute or migrate and their artifacts remain available.
- `/ralph-stop [run-id]` cancels correlated sessions and cleans up owned processes. Stale completions cannot advance the run.
- `/ralph-remove` previews removal of this checkout's `.ralph` and the repository's saved Ralph workflow state, then waits for approval in chat. It preserves project code, Git history, feature branches and worktrees. Active/unsettled work blocks removal; stop it first. Shared state removal affects linked worktrees. The global plugin stays installed for other projects and future setup.

For an existing run, `/ralph-status` adds `issueSummary` with eight numeric counts:
`total`, `pending`, `ready`, `running`, `integration_queued`, `integrated`, `blocked`
and `failed`. Only the selected run's `execution.issues` contributes: total is its
array length, and repeated statuses count individually. Other runs, planning issues
and issue attempts are excluded. Missing execution or an empty issues array yields
eight zeros. Explicit lookup and latest-run selection are unchanged, as are all
existing fields and no-run responses (which have no summary). Implementation:
`src/commands/router.ts`; public dispatch coverage: `tests/integration/01-plugin.test.ts`.

The dashboard URL includes an access token in its fragment. Open it locally; treat it as private. The listener uses loopback with an automatically allocated port, so independent repositories can run together. The browser is read-only; commands and approvals belong in OpenCode. State and write locks live under the repository's canonical Git common directory, `ralph/runs`, and are shared by linked worktrees. Independent repositories use independent stores. The plugin stays attached to the OpenCode server lifecycle; shutdown stops owned work and the dashboard. Restart reconciles facts and waits for explicit resume.

Setup, orchestrator and resume commands prompt the current assistant. Work runs in visible tool steps; questions return immediately and wait for a normal user reply. Workers and reviewers remain independent sessions. Commit approved project configuration and generated planning artifacts before approving execution so preflight has a clean checkout.

The v2 package ships server and TUI entry points. Status/control slash commands execute directly, emit a status notification, and queue their exact output as a synthetic inbox item without waking the model. `/ralph-commands` prompts the current model session to discover commands, ask for approval and use its save tool. The TUI follows the fresh interview and issue sessions and displays Ralph status notifications; the dashboard and persisted store remain authoritative.

The public `adapter`, `store`, `commands`, `types`, `review/*`, `reporting/completion` and `process` exports support typed host integration. The command router starts the same native stage workflow as slash commands. `continuePlanning` advances authorized native stages; `scheduleIssues` and `executeIssue` operate on the accepted DAG; `answerExecutorQuestion` validates correlated host-provided human answers. `reviewFeature` coordinates independent reviewers and the final report. Low-level `runJob` supports correlated session jobs. Tests and fixtures are excluded from runtime exports.

Code and functional reviewers load their bundled `skills/code-review` and `skills/functional-review` resources. Code-review options accept a `{ providerID, modelID }` model; functional review accepts its own model through `functionalOptions`. Executor jobs carry the same optional model field. Model/tool availability and OpenCode permissions remain host-owned; no permission is auto-approved. Command configuration validates lifecycle ordering, platform coverage (or explicit unsupported reasons), paths, timeouts, argument arrays, explicit shells and environment references. Role models and replacement skill paths are read from optional `.ralph/project.json` and captured with hashes on each run.

## Interview settings

Project replacements resolve relative to the workspace root. Omit `skillPaths.interview`
to use the original bundled grill-me guidance. Omit `models.interview` to capture the main
session's selected model; an unavailable selection produces an actionable error.

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "models": { "interview": { "providerID": "your-provider", "modelID": "your-model" } },
  "skillPaths": { "interview": "skills/my-interview.md" },
  "concurrency": 2,
  "timeoutMs": 300000,
  "agentTimeoutMs": 1800000
}
```

Runs record model selections, skill paths and content hashes, the verified command hash,
and timeout/concurrency defaults. Agent timeouts interrupt planning and retain the lock
and evidence. Human answers do not time out. `agentTimeoutMs` separately controls executor and merger deadlines (default thirty minutes), and is recorded on the run and each issue attempt. One canonical repository lock includes
planning; explanation approval retains it through execution. Terminal coordinated review releases the matching lock. Interrupted locks require reconciliation and explicit resume.

## Concurrent execution resources

Ready issues are selected in approved graph order. A submission waits in the integration
queue; children start only after all parents integrate, including qualified integration
with omitted checks. `/ralph-status` and durable events expose ready, pending, running,
integration_queued, integrated, failed and blocked states plus queue order. The executor
slot ends after submission cleanup; merger/validation work uses a separate serial queue.

`/ralph-commands` may propose resource allocation in the exact approved configuration:

```json
{
  "resources": {
    "RALPH_PORT": "port",
    "RALPH_NAMESPACE": "namespace",
    "RALPH_DIRECTORY": "directory"
  },
  "exclusiveResources": ["shared-test-database"]
}
```

Command `env` mappings reference these source names, for example
`{"PORT":"RALPH_PORT","APP_NAMESPACE":"RALPH_NAMESPACE","APP_DATA":"RALPH_DIRECTORY"}`.
Ports, namespaces and directories are allocated per lifecycle/job. Executor prompts
receive the allocation; candidates receive fresh allocations. The runner executes only
approved commands, using main-session permission before an executor session exists.
Shared resource names serialize use within the repository. Startup without allocated
ports also serializes use. Missing required references block setup; commands are never
invented or rewritten to obtain isolation. Owned cleanup releases only that lifecycle's
resources; cleanup failures retain ownership and diagnostics.

## Executor questions

An executor may return `{"kind":"needs_input","question":"...","recommendedAnswer":"...","affectedRequirement":"..."}`. Ralph persists the question on its run/issue/attempt and publishes it through the main-session adapter. The issue and descendants wait while independent issues continue. Human waiting consumes no executor capacity, agent deadline or retry. A correlated clarification resumes the same worktree and attempt with a new job identity and the remaining active-time budget.

Hosts answer through the adapter’s authenticated `UserInputResult` contract. The production plugin invokes native forms and binds answers to their request, session, revision and real human result. Stale requests, changed revisions, duplicate messages and unrelated sessions are rejected. Ordinary chat messages do not authorize feature execution.

For scope changes, use `ralph_request_product_decision` from the owning stage. Revised explanations require a newly displayed native scope authorization and invalidate dependent planning outputs. Executors that discover changed requirements return a correlated scope proposal with a revised explanation; accepted artifacts must be regenerated under renewed authorization. Integrated evidence is retained. There is no second graph or execution approval and no browser mutation endpoint.

## Validation semantics

Three **total issue attempts** include the first submission and two retries; an expected failing TDD red test does not consume an attempt. Three **total review rounds** apply to the final dual review; each generated repair task has its own three-attempt limit. Functional infrastructure retries have a separate three-attempt budget. Issue repair limits are enforced through the public single-issue entrypoint; the dependency scheduler preserves independent progress when an issue exhausts its budget.

The validation policy skips only timed-out validation checks for the current run, never setup/readiness timeouts, permission denials, cancellations or completed failures. Those omissions must remain in the report and must not rewrite approved commands. The baseline runs on an isolated clean checkout before implementation. Check timeouts are run-local omissions; setup failures and timeouts block readiness. Retain failed worktrees and their evidence for diagnosis; clean successful worktrees only after validated integration. Recovery does not silently discard retained resources.

A completed agent job is not proof of a verified feature. Reports distinguish `verified`, `incomplete-validation` (including skipped checks), `unverified` (missing review evidence) and `unresolved` (blocking issues/findings). Inspect the report's commit, checks, unavailable review and cleanup/resource fields. No workflow script publishes, pushes, creates a PR or merges into a target branch.

## Reproduce the package demonstration

```sh
bun test tests/integration/15-package.test.ts
bun run test:package
bun test tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts
bun run test:browser
bun run test:smoke
```

The package probe builds a clean distribution, packs it, installs it into two committed temporary repositories with spaces in their paths, and loads the installed plugin. It checks bundled skills/dashboard assets, separate stores, concurrent ports, argv preservation, readiness, cleanup and controls. Its deterministic workflow uses native stage services and completion tools, real Git/TDD/integration, independent reviewers, and a real timeout omission. Human answers and agent outputs are fixture supplied; these verdicts do not prove live native UI behavior. Temporary repositories are removed; `test:package` prints outcomes and blockers. Set `PACKAGE_EVIDENCE_PATH` to retain JSON evidence.

For the real installed OpenCode server probe, enable `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1` (PowerShell: `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'`) before `bun run test:smoke`. It uses a local deterministic model. The separate credentialed session probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL` and `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model`; without them it reports SKIPPED. The credentialed probe covers a model session, not the complete workflow; native interactive rendering and full live-workflow evidence remain separate verification requirements. A v2 server also requires authentication: set `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD` for the live probe. Credentials remain in the configured server; do not copy them into fixtures or package assets.

See [troubleshooting](docs/troubleshooting.md). CI runs the required suites on macOS, Ubuntu and native Windows and retains per-platform package evidence. A green fixture demo does not remove the production-host or unrun-platform blockers.

For a focused public status behavior loop, run `bun run test:status`. This selects integration tests whose names contain `status`; the full `bun run test` suite remains required for final validation.

Feature input uses native question forms; enable the host question tool. Setup and previewed project removal retain their ordinary-chat approval workflows. `ralph_workflow` and `/ralph-answer` are absent.

For a terminal `unverified` report caused by missing observations, `/ralph-resume <run-id> --review` retries review of the same clean feature commit within the existing three-round budget. Prior verdicts and issue attempts remain retained.

PRD and issue authoring each have three total attempts. Invalid completion arguments can be corrected in the current attempt; settled prose, failure or active-time timeout consumes one. Native questions and pause suspend authoring deadlines. Exhaustion requires an explicit native recovery choice to reset the budget or stop. PRD retries retain the interview session; issue retries use fresh reconciled sessions. Restarted discovery begins with a fresh session and opening question, preserving previous drafts and history.
# OpenCode compatibility

## Supported combination

This package targets **OpenCode 2.0.29**. Its server/TUI definitions and typed adapter use the v2 API; v1 hosts are no longer supported. Version 0.2.0 replaces the earlier 1.19.0 integration.

| Component | Exact version |
| --- | --- |
| `@opencode/cli` (development/test CLI) | 2.0.29 |
| `@opencode/plugin`, `@opencode/client`, `@opencode/schema` | 2.0.29 |
| Bun | 1.4.3 |
| TypeScript / Biome | 5.9.3 / 2.4.6 |

The real-server smoke probe builds, packs, installs and loads the package in an isolated Git repository. On macOS arm64 it exercises completed sessions, direct commands without model calls, event subscription, denied shell execution, linked-worktree observation restrictions, persistence, restart, explicit resume, interruption and status RPC. The deterministic local model proves the host boundary, not credentialed external-provider behavior. Native Linux/Windows results remain pending CI.

## Installation and entry points

Follow [README](../README.md). Configure `plugins` with the installed **package directory**, preferably a canonical, encoded file URL. Do not use the old `plugin` entry pointing to `dist/plugin.js`. OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js` from that directory; both have default definitions with `id: 'ralph'` and `setup(context)`. Package imports also expose `/server` and `/tui`.

Tests use `node_modules/@opencode/cli/bin/opencode.exe`, including on macOS/Linux. The manifest trusts this pinned CLI's postinstall script. Version changes require new runtime evidence, not just a dependency bump.

V2 activates external plugins asynchronously. Wait for command registration before dispatching external jobs; otherwise initial recovery may encounter newly recorded job intent. Local configuration changes require host activation/reload to complete.

Command registration can be visible before `plugin.list` publishes the active plugin. The installed-package probe also waits for that inventory entry before checking server/TUI features. This preserves the supported v2 mapping described in the [official plugin documentation](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins/); the ticket's proposed `@opencode-ai/plugin` import belongs to v1.

## Session adapter

`src/opencode/adapter.ts` accepts a narrow `AdapterHost`; `createAdapterClient` supplies it over the real v2 client and Ralph RPC. The plugin supplies the host's native session domain. V2 calls use flattened options and throw typed/transport failures rather than returning v1 `{ data, error, response }` envelopes.

```ts
const client = createAdapterClient(url, undefined, {
  Authorization: `Basic ${btoa('opencode:' + password)}`,
});
const session = await client.session.create({
  location: { directory }, title,
  model: { providerID, id: modelID },
});
const receipt = await client.session.prompt({ sessionID: session.id, text });
await client.session.wait({ sessionID: session.id });
const history = await client.session.context({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.interrupt({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.command({ sessionID: session.id, name: 'ralph-status', text: runId });
const commands = await client.command.list({ location: { directory } });
for await (const event of client.event.subscribe({ signal })) { /* v2 event.data */ }
```

Job intent is durable before session creation; the returned session ID is durable before prompting. Workspace selection occurs at session creation; subsequent calls target that session ID. Model fields map from Ralph's `{ providerID, modelID }` contract to v2 `{ providerID, id }`.

Prompt acknowledgment is not completion. The adapter waits, finds the delivered user message with the receipt's ID, and checks the subsequent turn. Success requires completed assistant text, `finish: 'stop'`, a successful idle record and no failed tools or session errors. Unexpected subsequent input makes correlation uncertain and returns missing output. Earlier failed/denied tools, including caught code-mode failures in tool metadata, cannot be erased by later assistant success.

Cancellation is recorded before interruption. The current operation journal uses the internal `session.abort` operation kind; v2 uses `session.interrupt` underneath. A late-created cancelled session is interrupted without prompting. Cancellation is checked again after prompt delivery so a prompt racing the first interrupt is interrupted again. Stale results cannot update superseded attempts.

## Commands, observations and notifications

Commands register with `context.command.transform`; tools register with `context.tool.transform`. Status/pause/resume/stop execute directly. The exact command output is queued as a synthetic inbox item with `resume: false`, and a status event notifies the TUI without waking the model. Pending output can be inspected with `client.session.inbox.list`; it is consumed on the next session execution. Status never depends on generated assistant text.

V2 does not expose the plugin tool catalog as a public client endpoint. `src/opencode/bridge.ts` defines location-scoped Ralph RPC for tool discovery, observation preparation and status notification. Review sessions expose only `ralph_review_*` aliases of permitted observation tools. Aliases retain each original tool's permission action and execution function. Context filtering hides mutation tools and code-mode `execute`; the execution hook also rejects unapproved calls. Normal sessions retain their original tools and hide the observation aliases. Host denials are never changed to allows and no permissions are automatically approved.

The TUI entry follows location-scoped stage-session events and shows informational status toasts; cleanup unsubscribes. Server-only consumers can subscribe to RPC events and use durable status/dashboard data. Feature answers use the host’s native question forms. Fake-host tests verify contracts and navigation deduplication; installed native rendering and cross-platform evidence for this stage flow remain separate.

## Recovery and remaining limits

The canonical Git common directory owns `<git-common-dir>/ralph/runs`. Its fsynced JSONL journal remains authoritative; snapshots use atomic rename and writers use ownership-verified locks. Linked worktrees share state. Multiple v2 locations in one server recover that state once and only the last location's cleanup stops shared work. A filesystem host lease coordinates isolated module graphs; another live server fails closed and a proven dead PID birth identity permits fresh recovery. Restart still requires explicit resume and never resets attempts or review budgets. Only native-interview-v1 feature records can resume. Earlier feature protocols are unsupported and never migrated; preserve their artifacts and settle ownership before starting a fresh workflow.

Ralph starts feature stages directly and uses native question forms for feature input and one scope authorization. The full grill-me skill runs in a fresh interview; create-prd runs in that session after approval; the full adapted to-issues skill runs in a fresh session. `ralph_complete_interview`, `ralph_complete_prd` and `ralph_complete_issues` validate owner/session/attempt and accepted artifacts. Issue completion starts execution automatically. Progress and results target the issue-planning chat. There is no chat reply transport or second execution approval. Setup saving and previewed removal retain ordinary-chat approvals.

PRD and issue authoring have three total attempts each; native question waits and pause suspend active-time deadlines. Exhaustion requires explicit native intervention. Recovery retains accepted bytes and remaining budgets; pre-PRD discovery restarts fresh. Fake-host integration tests establish these contracts, not live form rendering. Historical probes below describe earlier revisions; installed-host and cross-platform stage-flow evidence belongs to issue 25. Standalone Bun must remain on PATH because compiled OpenCode’s executable is not a general-purpose Bun runtime.

## Reproduce

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run test:status
bun run test
OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke
bun run test:package
```

On PowerShell set `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'` before running the smoke suite. The local probe uses an isolated test-only Basic-auth password. The separate live probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model` and the server's `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD`; without its opt-in/endpoint/model it is skipped. Do not put credentials in package resources or fixture data.

## Issue 1 verification — 2026-10-14

- macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3: typecheck and lint passed; all 19 issue-1 integration tests passed.
- `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun test tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts`: **2 passed, 1 skipped**. The fresh-source install uses only the manifest/lockfile and an empty package cache. The installed-package probe builds, packs and loads Ralph in real OpenCode 2.0.29, correlates its local model response through the public router/adapter, preserves permission denial, observes events and verifies status after host restart.
- Red/green evidence: the empty-cache install failed with `ENOENT extracting tarball from opencode-ralph-plugin` before removing the accidental self-dependency from the manifest and regenerating the lockfile. It then passed. A warm cache had concealed the missing archive. Package installation must not depend on a previously built copy of itself.
- The real probe initially failed on an unpublished plugin inventory entry; the probe now waits for active inventory independently of command registration, retaining its TUI assertion.
- Fresh-install checks are gated because they download dependencies; the deterministic issue-1 suite requires neither network model access nor paid calls. Credentialed external-provider execution was skipped. Linux and Windows have CI jobs but were not run here. Native human answers and the terminal answer dialog were subsequently verified in the separate live trial.

## Earlier packaged fixture verification — 2026-10-15

macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3 and the pinned OpenCode 2.0.29 combination passed the installed host smoke (3 passed across the smoke suite, 1 credentialed-provider skip), complete deterministic two-consumer setup-to-report test, six Chromium checks, typecheck/lint and the integration suite (194 passed, 1 separately gated browser smoke skipped). The demo performs real Git/TDD/integration and a real timeout omission; human responses and agent prose are supplied at the public adapter boundary. Full results, reproducible commands and remaining production/native-platform blockers are recorded in [package validation](package-validation.md). No Linux, native Windows or credentialed full-workflow success is claimed by these local results.

Historical verification above predates the native stage flow. The current TUI follows stage sessions and shows scoped status; native question results settle feature requests. Worker/reviewer sessions and worktrees remain independent.

## Historical credentialed verification

The separate live macOS feature trial completed with verified code and functional review on the same frozen commit, native test receipts, no workflow validation skips and clean cleanup. See [live trial](live-opencode-trial.md) and [machine-readable evidence](live-opencode-evidence.json). Earlier fixture counts and provider skips above describe their specific runs.
# Installation and operational troubleshooting

Use the exact dependency versions listed in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md) and Bun from the package manifest (`.bun-version` in a source checkout). Run the package probe from the source checkout: `bun run test:package`.

| Symptom | Check or action |
| --- | --- |
| Tarball install mentions a developer's home directory | Rebuild the current manifest; it no longer contains a self-dependency. Copy the archive into the consumer and install `./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz`. Avoid percent-encoded absolute tarball URLs with Bun 1.4.3. |
| Plugin fails to load on a path with spaces or Windows | Generate the canonical package-directory URL with `realpathSync` and `pathToFileURL` using the README command. Use the encoded URL for plugin loading; do not hand-build drive-letter file URLs. |
| Slash command missing | Restart the pinned OpenCode server in the correct repository and inspect `plugins` in its local `opencode.json`. V2 activates external plugins asynchronously; allow activation to finish. All seven Ralph commands are registered. Setup uses the current chat; orchestrator opens a fresh interview session and native question. |
| Wrapper prints the OpenCode version or exits before result collection | Rebuild this version and keep standalone Bun on PATH. Wrappers must launch Bun rather than the compiled host executable. |
| Proposal rejects an empty unsupportedPlatforms map | Rebuild this version; the platform-exclusion map is partial. |
| Global OpenCode behaves differently | Use OpenCode 2.0.29. This package uses the v2 plugin contract; v1 is no longer supported. |
| Local test CLI says postinstall was not run | Run `bun pm trust @opencode/cli`; the manifest trusts only this pinned CLI install script. |
| HTTP SDK returns 401 | Pass the v2 server Basic-auth header to `createAdapterClient(url, fetch, headers)`. The smoke test uses an isolated test-only password. |
| Dashboard inaccessible | Get a fresh URL from `/ralph-status`. Use its loopback host and fragment token. Tokens are process-scoped and change on server restart. The listener stops when the plugin is disposed. |
| Dashboard returns 401/403/405 | Authentication is required for API reads. Cross-origin and mutation requests are rejected. Use OpenCode for controls. |
| Reopened workflow does not continue | Recovery intentionally waits for `/ralph-resume`. Read the recovery facts first; resolve blocked/conflicting Git, session or ownership evidence. Attempts and revision budgets do not reset. |
| Opening an executor worktree cancels its parent run | Use this version: repository host ownership is shared through the Git common directory across isolated plugin locations. |
| Another live OpenCode host owns this repository | Close that repository in the other host before activating Ralph here. A live owner is never stolen; stale birth identities recover on the next activation. |
| Writer lock or process ownership cannot be inspected | Do not delete state or kill arbitrary PIDs. Recovery only reclaims provably dead local owners. Native Windows requires PowerShell/CIM and `taskkill.exe`; POSIX requires `ps` and process-group signals. Unknown ownership is a visible blocker. Network-filesystem lock atomicity is not promised. |
| Completion says incomplete/unverified | Read skipped checks, unavailable criteria/review, failed cleanup and retained resources in the report. A successful assistant response does not validate checks. |
| Failed worktree remains | Keep it with its evidence for diagnosis. Do not remove a worktree referenced by an active operation. Successful issue cleanup is owned by integration, not package installation. |
| Live smoke is skipped | The credentialed probe requires opt-in, endpoint, model and v2 server authentication documented in README. A deterministic local model or fake adapter cannot prove provider compatibility. |
| Linux or Windows result is unavailable | Run the corresponding native CI matrix job. Missing or failed supported-platform evidence remains a release blocker. macOS results cannot certify Windows cleanup behavior. |

No install or release script pushes, publishes, creates a PR or merges a feature into its target. The two-repository demonstration uses local temporary Git branches only. Runtime state is under the Git common directory; logs, repositories, test fixtures and secrets are excluded from the package's allowlisted contents. The build removes stale `dist` output before compilation.

### A Ralph question appears without a terminal answer control

Feature questions and scope approval use the host’s native question tool. Enable it and inspect `/ralph-status` for the pending request and owning stage. `/ralph-orchestrator` opens a fresh interview; accepted PRD completion opens a fresh planner. Execution progress and the report appear in the planner chat. Ordinary chat replies cannot approve a feature. Setup saving and removal still use chat approval. `ralph_workflow` and `/ralph-answer` are removed.

Authoring has three total attempts per PRD/issue stage. Pending native questions and pause suspend the deadline. Exhaustion asks natively whether to reset the budget or stop. Before accepted PRD recovery offers fresh discovery; later recovery preserves accepted bytes and remaining budgets. Unsupported saved protocols require a fresh workflow with preserved artifacts, never migration. Changed starting commit or commands require renewed explanation approval, not graph approval.

### Review cannot verify historical TDD

New executor jobs retain native test-tool receipts for final review. A terminal `unverified` report may be retried explicitly with `/ralph-resume <run-id> --review` when the feature commit and approved commands are unchanged and cleanup completed. This consumes another review round and preserves the original report in the journal. Missing history remains missing evidence, never an inferred pass.

### Correct directory, but Ralph commands are still missing

Restarting the OpenCode terminal UI can reconnect to the same long-running background service. After changing a local plugin path, that service can report the new configuration while retaining an active plugin from the old path and exposing no Ralph commands. Compare the authenticated native `plugin.list({location})` source with the configured package path and inspect `command.list({location})` for the exact repository.

Refreshing the idle repository location with the authenticated SDK’s `client.debug.location.evict({location: {directory}})` forces that location to load the current package. Wait for its active plugin and command inventory afterward. This refreshes the selected repository rather than terminating the shared service and other projects. A live reproduction on the ordinary user service loaded the old `node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin` copy despite the new global config; after refresh, all six commands appeared in the API and in plain `opencode` slash autocomplete.

### Discovery reports a missing guessed search directory

Do not create `src/project` to satisfy a discovery guess. Current command discovery tolerates missing read-only search paths and can continue to a valid proposal. Permission denials and other tool failures still block. The current assistant can correct an exploratory search and continue. `/ralph-commands` uses normal chat approval and the validated save tool; it no longer depends on a separate discovery session.

### Remove Ralph from a project
Run `/ralph-remove`, review the exact deletion preview, and approve in chat. It removes `.ralph` and saved workflow state for the repository, preserving code, Git history, feature branches and workspaces. Linked worktrees share that saved state. Active workers or unsettled cleanup block deletion; use `/ralph-stop` and resolve the listed facts before requesting a new preview. The globally installed plugin and active host bookkeeping are retained.
# Workflow controls and recovery

## Summary
OpenCode registers pause/resume/stop commands and invokes reconciliation on restart.
Recovery observes actual state, reports facts, and waits for explicit resume. Native orchestrator/resume invoke stage services directly; workers and reviewers keep their independent sessions and worktrees.

## Key behavior
- Native `/ralph-orchestrator` offers correlated existing-run choices through the native question adapter. A run without an accepted PRD offers fresh discovery or retention; accepted planning offers saved-stage resume, retention, and restart while execution has not begun. Unsupported protocols require a fresh workflow and are not migrated.
- Pre-PRD recovery cancels and inspects unfinished interview/PRD jobs; it records settled unfinished work rather than requiring completion evidence for a draft that will not be accepted. Unconfirmed cancellation or busy/unknown sessions retain the blocker.
- Discovery restart stops and inspects the recorded stage sessions before releasing the exact repository/run owner through a durable release intent/result. Cleanup uncertainty, foreign locks, pending operations and execution work block release. History, drafts and worktrees remain; released runs cannot resume. A fresh interview starts with its opening question and empty transcript.
- Native `/ralph-resume` continues saved stage ownership directly. Reactivation precedes guarded planning writes; it does not rebind a native owner into the invoking chat. Accepted PRD/graph intents finish immutable publication and the necessary handoff or execution boundary. Execution continuation occurs once when acceptance finalization already started it. Resuming completed integrations can collect the existing executor turn for final-review evidence; this observation must not create or prompt another worker.
- Reconciliation verifies accepted PRD bytes and scope authorization, repository ownership and stable host prompt receipts. A supported live authoring attempt with correlated delivery retains its owner and remaining budget through recovery freeze; stopped/blocked owners stay retired. Unknown delivery evidence blocks repeated dispatch. Recovery remains observational until explicit resume.
- Native PRD and issue authoring each allow three total attempts per approved stage, independent of issue/review budgets. Only accepted completion tools advance the stage. A settled turn with final prose, failed turn, or active-time timeout consumes an attempt; correctable completion validation stays in the live attempt.
- Authoring attempts persist the owning session, prompt identity, draft root, captured remaining active-time budget and terminal result. Pending product/approval questions, direct native question tools and pause suspend the deadline. The injected planning clock supports deterministic deadline checks.
- Failure retires tool authority before cancelling the recorded planning job. Retry requires confirmed cancellation and an idle/missing inspected owner; missing PRD interview sessions require intervention. PRD retries retain the same interview session; issue retries use reconciled fresh sessions. Each retry writes a separate draft namespace and receives accepted inputs, decision history, prior draft paths and the failure reason. Retired sessions retain their planning tool restrictions.
- Exhaustion retains the blocker and native recovery request. Only a correlated real native “Reset budget and retry” answer grants another three attempts, with answer provenance recorded in `budgetResets`; “Stop” stops the run. Unavailable native input leaves the request pending and publishes its diagnostic. Stop/pause racing cancellation cannot allocate another attempt. Accepted completion atomically terminates the planning job, so its later turn settlement cannot cancel execution or consume another attempt. Authorized scope transitions retire superseded authoring jobs without aborting the continuing session; obsolete jobs cannot block final review.
- `/ralph-remove` previews deletion of the checkout's `.ralph` and repository-shared saved workflow state. A new user approval is required. It checks the unchanged preview under setup/write locks and refuses active/unsettled jobs or uncertain live processes. It preserves code, branches and workspaces, including unregistered retained workspace directories. Linked-worktree scope is disclosed. Live host bookkeeping remains while the plugin is loaded, allowing a later setup in the same session.
- `/ralph-resume` and `ralph_control` share the typed router resume service. Without an ID, it selects the latest feature ahead of unrelated setup records. `--base <commit>`/`selectedCommit` requests renewed explanation authorization; `--review`/`review` retains explicit review-only retry after terminal ownership release. Options cannot be combined, and unknown flags are rejected.
- Native resume drives the saved phase; it does more than flip the control flag. Pending planning, failed baseline retry, scheduling, and automatic review continuation retain approvals and attempt budgets.
- Pause rejects new jobs and suspends review scheduling while current jobs settle. Production executor submissions retain their attempt identity; mergers and successful in-flight checks wait at the next scheduling/ref boundary rather than becoming failed retries. Each resumed in-flight boundary rechecks the original epoch so stop followed by resume cannot revive a stale callback.
- Stop cancels bound sessions, owned processes and registered environments with bounded cleanup. Late job results cannot advance stopped runs.
- Process ownership requires PID birth identity, owner token, repository and workspace. Owned wrappers watch parent liveness; the dashboard stays in the plugin process.
- Recovery shares the Git common-directory store across worktrees. Already advanced integration intents require exact SHA/check revision evidence and record completion once. Ambiguous operations block resume; validation without output never passes.
- Stop retains existing recovery/cleanup blockers, including repeated stops. Resume cannot race pending stop cleanup. Reconciliation must resolve retained ambiguity.
- Recovery binds production validation to the exact merger envelope, candidate, command hash/revision and approval. It restores the accepted issue result even if the ref or operation result was written before a crash. A proven old index/clean worktree is synchronized with a safe two-tree merge; local changes block recovery.
- Review counters, attempt identities, approvals and artifact revisions are retained; controls do not reset budgets. Native resume preserves the stage owner and correlated unanswered questions.
- Recovery holds a pending control marker through inspection and checks its epoch/revision before publishing. It verifies logical active-run lock identity and the latest accepted refs; uncertain execution/merge/repair completion blocks instead of repeating dispatch.
- An executing attempt with no submission/candidate can be recovered as a consumed failure only after a matching cancelled job, completed abort, idle/missing exact session and proven environment cleanup. Work and attempt history remain available to the next explicit retry. Busy, unknown or mismatched sessions stay blocked.
- Journal locks are replaced only after inspecting their recorded owner birth identity. Missing or corrupt owner evidence does not authorize replacement.

- Ordinary-chat feature receipts and question suppression are removed. `ralph_control resume`, router and slash commands use native stage services. Setup saving and previewed removal retain separate approval semantics; removal receipts are owned by `src/commands/removal-approval.ts`. Unsupported records require fresh workflow creation without migration or deleting their artifacts.

## Code references
- `src/commands/orchestrator.ts`, `src/commands/resume.ts`, `src/plugin.ts` — native existing-run selection, fresh restart and saved-stage continuation.
- `src/state/active-run.ts`, `src/commands/control.ts`, `src/state/run-store.ts` — matching stopped-planning release, inspected stage cancellation and guarded reactivation.
- `tests/integration/14-recovery.test.ts` — native first-question restart, uncertain cleanup, foreign/released ownership, immutable PRD tampering, ambiguous prompt evidence, five PRD handoff crash boundaries and three issue acceptance/eligibility boundaries. Real temporary Git projects with deterministic fake-host evidence; not installed native UI proof.
- `src/planning/generation.ts`, `src/planning/types.ts` — durable authoring attempts, bounded retries, native exhaustion recovery and injectable supervision clock.
- `src/opencode/adapter.ts`, `src/plugin.ts` — prompt-specific turn inspection, owned cancellation, native question waits, periodic supervision and retired-session tool confinement.
- `src/planning/completion.ts`, `src/planning/artifact-writer.ts`, `src/state/run-store.ts` — revision-checked acceptance/writes, retirement rejection and terminal planning jobs before downstream handoffs.
- `tests/integration/04-planning.test.ts` — real Git/fake-host cases for three-attempt exhaustion/reset, retained drafts, correctable validation, suspended deadlines, uncertain cancellation, stop/pause races and accepted handoffs. These are deterministic host fixtures, not proof of live native UI behavior.
- `src/commands/remove-project.ts` — previewed project removal preserving code/branches/worktrees.
- `src/tui.ts`, `tests/integration/tui-input.test.ts` — stage navigation and status TUI without custom answer dialogs.
- `tests/integration/chat-workflow.test.ts`, `tests/integration/remove-project.test.ts`, `tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts` — absence of legacy registration, native question availability, unsupported resume rejection and preserved removal approval. Smoke evidence is separate and historical.
- `src/commands/resume.ts`, `src/plugin.ts` — native resume continuation and guarded starting-commit selection.
- `src/commands/control.ts` — dispatch gates and bounded stop cleanup.
- `src/recovery/reconcile.ts`, `src/state/run-store.ts` — facts, guarded recovered-attempt writes and idempotent operation reconciliation.
- `src/git/worktrees.ts` — safe synchronization after a ref update interrupted checkout synchronization.
- `src/core/orchestrator.ts`, `src/execution/execute-issue.ts`, `src/execution/integrate-issue.ts`, `src/execution/validation.ts`, `src/commands/router.ts` — production pause boundaries and resumed question publication.
- `src/process/ownership.ts`, `src/process/owned-child.ts` — owned process inspection and parent monitoring.
- `src/plugin.ts`, `src/state/host-lease.ts` — filesystem host lease shared across isolated OpenCode location modules; only first activation recovers and last disposal stops work. Another live host fails closed; dead birth identities permit recovery.
- `tests/integration/14-recovery.test.ts` — real Git recovery and lifecycle regressions.

