# Installation and operational troubleshooting

Use the exact dependency versions listed in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md) and Bun from the package manifest (`.bun-version` in a source checkout). Run the package probe from the source checkout: `bun run test:package`.

| Symptom | Check or action |
| --- | --- |
| Tarball install mentions a developer's home directory | Rebuild the current manifest; it no longer contains a self-dependency. Copy the archive into the consumer and install `./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz`. Avoid percent-encoded absolute tarball URLs with Bun 1.4.3. |
| Plugin fails to load on a path with spaces or Windows | Generate the canonical package-directory URL with `realpathSync` and `pathToFileURL` using the README command. Use the encoded URL for plugin loading; do not hand-build drive-letter file URLs. |
| Slash command missing | Restart the pinned OpenCode server in the correct repository and inspect `plugins` in its local `opencode.json`. V2 activates external plugins asynchronously; allow activation to finish. All seven Ralph commands are registered. Setup uses the current chat; orchestrator opens a fresh interview session and native question. |
| Wrapper prints the OpenCode version or exits before result collection | Rebuild this version and keep standalone Bun on PATH. Wrappers must launch Bun rather than the compiled host executable. |
| Proposal rejects an empty unsupportedPlatforms map | Rebuild this version; the platform-exclusion map is partial. |
| Global OpenCode behaves differently | Use OpenCode 2.0.29. This package uses the v2 plugin contract; v1 is no longer supported. |
| Local test CLI says postinstall was not run | Run `bun pm trust @opencode/cli`; the manifest trusts only this pinned CLI install script. |
| HTTP SDK returns 401 | Pass the v2 server Basic-auth header to `createAdapterClient(url, fetch, headers)`. The smoke test uses an isolated test-only password. |
| Dashboard inaccessible | Get a fresh URL from `/ralph-status`. Use its loopback host and fragment token. Tokens are process-scoped and change on server restart. The listener stops when the plugin is disposed. |
| Dashboard returns 401/403/405 | Authentication is required for API reads. Cross-origin and mutation requests are rejected. Use OpenCode for controls. |
| Reopened workflow does not continue | Recovery intentionally waits for `/ralph-resume`. Read the recovery facts first; resolve blocked/conflicting Git, session or ownership evidence. Attempts and revision budgets do not reset. |
| Opening an executor worktree cancels its parent run | Use this version: repository host ownership is shared through the Git common directory across isolated plugin locations. |
| Another live OpenCode host owns this repository | Close that repository in the other host before activating Ralph here. A live owner is never stolen; stale birth identities recover on the next activation. |
| Writer lock or process ownership cannot be inspected | Do not delete state or kill arbitrary PIDs. Recovery only reclaims provably dead local owners. Native Windows requires PowerShell/CIM and `taskkill.exe`; POSIX requires `ps` and process-group signals. Unknown ownership is a visible blocker. Network-filesystem lock atomicity is not promised. |
| Completion says incomplete/unverified | Read skipped checks, unavailable criteria/review, failed cleanup and retained resources in the report. A successful assistant response does not validate checks. |
| Failed worktree remains | Keep it with its evidence for diagnosis. Do not remove a worktree referenced by an active operation. Successful issue cleanup is owned by integration, not package installation. |
| Live smoke is skipped | The credentialed probe requires opt-in, endpoint, model and v2 server authentication documented in README. A deterministic local model or fake adapter cannot prove provider compatibility. |
| Linux or Windows result is unavailable | Run the corresponding native CI matrix job. Missing or failed supported-platform evidence remains a release blocker. macOS results cannot certify Windows cleanup behavior. |

No install or release script pushes, publishes, creates a PR or merges a feature into its target. The two-repository demonstration uses local temporary Git branches only. Runtime state is under the Git common directory; logs, repositories, test fixtures and secrets are excluded from the package's allowlisted contents. The build removes stale `dist` output before compilation.

### A Ralph question appears without a terminal answer control

Feature questions and scope approval use the host’s native question tool. Enable it and inspect `/ralph-status` for the pending request and owning stage. `/ralph-orchestrator` opens a fresh interview; accepted PRD completion opens a fresh planner. Execution progress and the report appear in the planner chat. Ordinary chat replies cannot approve a feature. Setup saving and removal still use chat approval. `ralph_workflow` and `/ralph-answer` are removed.

Authoring has three total attempts per PRD/issue stage. Pending native questions and pause suspend the deadline. Exhaustion asks natively whether to reset the budget or stop. Before accepted PRD recovery offers fresh discovery; later recovery preserves accepted bytes and remaining budgets. Unsupported saved protocols require a fresh workflow with preserved artifacts, never migration. Changed starting commit or commands require renewed explanation approval, not graph approval.

### Review cannot verify historical TDD

New executor jobs retain native test-tool receipts for final review. A terminal `unverified` report may be retried explicitly with `/ralph-resume <run-id> --review` when the feature commit and approved commands are unchanged and cleanup completed. This consumes another review round and preserves the original report in the journal. Missing history remains missing evidence, never an inferred pass.

### Correct directory, but Ralph commands are still missing

Restarting the OpenCode terminal UI can reconnect to the same long-running background service. After changing a local plugin path, that service can report the new configuration while retaining an active plugin from the old path and exposing no Ralph commands. Compare the authenticated native `plugin.list({location})` source with the configured package path and inspect `command.list({location})` for the exact repository.

Refreshing the idle repository location with the authenticated SDK’s `client.debug.location.evict({location: {directory}})` forces that location to load the current package. Wait for its active plugin and command inventory afterward. This refreshes the selected repository rather than terminating the shared service and other projects. A live reproduction on the ordinary user service loaded the old `node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin` copy despite the new global config; after refresh, all six commands appeared in the API and in plain `opencode` slash autocomplete.

### Discovery reports a missing guessed search directory

Do not create `src/project` to satisfy a discovery guess. Current command discovery tolerates missing read-only search paths and can continue to a valid proposal. Permission denials and other tool failures still block. The current assistant can correct an exploratory search and continue. `/ralph-commands` uses normal chat approval and the validated save tool; it no longer depends on a separate discovery session.

### Remove Ralph from a project
Run `/ralph-remove`, review the exact deletion preview, and approve in chat. It removes `.ralph` and saved workflow state for the repository, preserving code, Git history, feature branches and workspaces. Linked worktrees share that saved state. Active workers or unsettled cleanup block deletion; use `/ralph-stop` and resolve the listed facts before requesting a new preview. The globally installed plugin and active host bookkeeping are retained.
# OpenCode compatibility

## Supported combination

This package targets **OpenCode 2.0.29**. Its server/TUI definitions and typed adapter use the v2 API; v1 hosts are no longer supported. Version 0.2.0 replaces the earlier 1.19.0 integration.

| Component | Exact version |
| --- | --- |
| `@opencode/cli` (development/test CLI) | 2.0.29 |
| `@opencode/plugin`, `@opencode/client`, `@opencode/schema` | 2.0.29 |
| Bun | 1.4.3 |
| TypeScript / Biome | 5.9.3 / 2.4.6 |

The real-server smoke probe builds, packs, installs and loads the package in an isolated Git repository. On macOS arm64 it exercises completed sessions, direct commands without model calls, event subscription, denied shell execution, linked-worktree observation restrictions, persistence, restart, explicit resume, interruption and status RPC. The deterministic local model proves the host boundary, not credentialed external-provider behavior. Native Linux/Windows results remain pending CI.

## Installation and entry points

Follow [README](../README.md). Configure `plugins` with the installed **package directory**, preferably a canonical, encoded file URL. Do not use the old `plugin` entry pointing to `dist/plugin.js`. OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js` from that directory; both have default definitions with `id: 'ralph'` and `setup(context)`. Package imports also expose `/server` and `/tui`.

Tests use `node_modules/@opencode/cli/bin/opencode.exe`, including on macOS/Linux. The manifest trusts this pinned CLI's postinstall script. Version changes require new runtime evidence, not just a dependency bump.

V2 activates external plugins asynchronously. Wait for command registration before dispatching external jobs; otherwise initial recovery may encounter newly recorded job intent. Local configuration changes require host activation/reload to complete.

Command registration can be visible before `plugin.list` publishes the active plugin. The installed-package probe also waits for that inventory entry before checking server/TUI features. This preserves the supported v2 mapping described in the [official plugin documentation](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins/); the ticket's proposed `@opencode-ai/plugin` import belongs to v1.

## Session adapter

`src/opencode/adapter.ts` accepts a narrow `AdapterHost`; `createAdapterClient` supplies it over the real v2 client and Ralph RPC. The plugin supplies the host's native session domain. V2 calls use flattened options and throw typed/transport failures rather than returning v1 `{ data, error, response }` envelopes.

```ts
const client = createAdapterClient(url, undefined, {
  Authorization: `Basic ${btoa('opencode:' + password)}`,
});
const session = await client.session.create({
  location: { directory }, title,
  model: { providerID, id: modelID },
});
const receipt = await client.session.prompt({ sessionID: session.id, text });
await client.session.wait({ sessionID: session.id });
const history = await client.session.context({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.interrupt({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.command({ sessionID: session.id, name: 'ralph-status', text: runId });
const commands = await client.command.list({ location: { directory } });
for await (const event of client.event.subscribe({ signal })) { /* v2 event.data */ }
```

Job intent is durable before session creation; the returned session ID is durable before prompting. Workspace selection occurs at session creation; subsequent calls target that session ID. Model fields map from Ralph's `{ providerID, modelID }` contract to v2 `{ providerID, id }`.

Prompt acknowledgment is not completion. The adapter waits, finds the delivered user message with the receipt's ID, and checks the subsequent turn. Success requires completed assistant text, `finish: 'stop'`, a successful idle record and no failed tools or session errors. Unexpected subsequent input makes correlation uncertain and returns missing output. Earlier failed/denied tools, including caught code-mode failures in tool metadata, cannot be erased by later assistant success.

Cancellation is recorded before interruption. The current operation journal uses the internal `session.abort` operation kind; v2 uses `session.interrupt` underneath. A late-created cancelled session is interrupted without prompting. Cancellation is checked again after prompt delivery so a prompt racing the first interrupt is interrupted again. Stale results cannot update superseded attempts.

## Commands, observations and notifications

Commands register with `context.command.transform`; tools register with `context.tool.transform`. Status/pause/resume/stop execute directly. The exact command output is queued as a synthetic inbox item with `resume: false`, and a status event notifies the TUI without waking the model. Pending output can be inspected with `client.session.inbox.list`; it is consumed on the next session execution. Status never depends on generated assistant text.

V2 does not expose the plugin tool catalog as a public client endpoint. `src/opencode/bridge.ts` defines location-scoped Ralph RPC for tool discovery, observation preparation and status notification. Review sessions expose only `ralph_review_*` aliases of permitted observation tools. Aliases retain each original tool's permission action and execution function. Context filtering hides mutation tools and code-mode `execute`; the execution hook also rejects unapproved calls. Normal sessions retain their original tools and hide the observation aliases. Host denials are never changed to allows and no permissions are automatically approved.

The TUI entry follows location-scoped stage-session events and shows informational status toasts; cleanup unsubscribes. Server-only consumers can subscribe to RPC events and use durable status/dashboard data. Feature answers use the host’s native question forms. Fake-host tests verify contracts and navigation deduplication; installed native rendering and cross-platform evidence for this stage flow remain separate.

## Recovery and remaining limits

The canonical Git common directory owns `<git-common-dir>/ralph/runs`. Its fsynced JSONL journal remains authoritative; snapshots use atomic rename and writers use ownership-verified locks. Linked worktrees share state. Multiple v2 locations in one server recover that state once and only the last location's cleanup stops shared work. A filesystem host lease coordinates isolated module graphs; another live server fails closed and a proven dead PID birth identity permits fresh recovery. Restart still requires explicit resume and never resets attempts or review budgets. Only native-interview-v1 feature records can resume. Earlier feature protocols are unsupported and never migrated; preserve their artifacts and settle ownership before starting a fresh workflow.

Ralph starts feature stages directly and uses native question forms for feature input and one scope authorization. The full grill-me skill runs in a fresh interview; create-prd runs in that session after approval; the full adapted to-issues skill runs in a fresh session. `ralph_complete_interview`, `ralph_complete_prd` and `ralph_complete_issues` validate owner/session/attempt and accepted artifacts. Issue completion starts execution automatically. Progress and results target the issue-planning chat. There is no chat reply transport or second execution approval. Setup saving and previewed removal retain ordinary-chat approvals.

PRD and issue authoring have three total attempts each; native question waits and pause suspend active-time deadlines. Exhaustion requires explicit native intervention. Recovery retains accepted bytes and remaining budgets; pre-PRD discovery restarts fresh. Fake-host integration tests establish these contracts, not live form rendering. Historical probes below describe earlier revisions; installed-host and cross-platform stage-flow evidence belongs to issue 25. Standalone Bun must remain on PATH because compiled OpenCode’s executable is not a general-purpose Bun runtime.

## Reproduce

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run test:status
bun run test
OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke
bun run test:package
```

On PowerShell set `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'` before running the smoke suite. The local probe uses an isolated test-only Basic-auth password. The separate live probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model` and the server's `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD`; without its opt-in/endpoint/model it is skipped. Do not put credentials in package resources or fixture data.

## Issue 1 verification — 2026-10-14

- macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3: typecheck and lint passed; all 19 issue-1 integration tests passed.
- `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun test tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts`: **2 passed, 1 skipped**. The fresh-source install uses only the manifest/lockfile and an empty package cache. The installed-package probe builds, packs and loads Ralph in real OpenCode 2.0.29, correlates its local model response through the public router/adapter, preserves permission denial, observes events and verifies status after host restart.
- Red/green evidence: the empty-cache install failed with `ENOENT extracting tarball from opencode-ralph-plugin` before removing the accidental self-dependency from the manifest and regenerating the lockfile. It then passed. A warm cache had concealed the missing archive. Package installation must not depend on a previously built copy of itself.
- The real probe initially failed on an unpublished plugin inventory entry; the probe now waits for active inventory independently of command registration, retaining its TUI assertion.
- Fresh-install checks are gated because they download dependencies; the deterministic issue-1 suite requires neither network model access nor paid calls. Credentialed external-provider execution was skipped. Linux and Windows have CI jobs but were not run here. Native human answers and the terminal answer dialog were subsequently verified in the separate live trial.

## Earlier packaged fixture verification — 2026-10-15

macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3 and the pinned OpenCode 2.0.29 combination passed the installed host smoke (3 passed across the smoke suite, 1 credentialed-provider skip), complete deterministic two-consumer setup-to-report test, six Chromium checks, typecheck/lint and the integration suite (194 passed, 1 separately gated browser smoke skipped). The demo performs real Git/TDD/integration and a real timeout omission; human responses and agent prose are supplied at the public adapter boundary. Full results, reproducible commands and remaining production/native-platform blockers are recorded in [package validation](package-validation.md). No Linux, native Windows or credentialed full-workflow success is claimed by these local results.

Historical verification above predates the native stage flow. The current TUI follows stage sessions and shows scoped status; native question results settle feature requests. Worker/reviewer sessions and worktrees remain independent.

## Historical credentialed verification

The separate live macOS feature trial completed with verified code and functional review on the same frozen commit, native test receipts, no workflow validation skips and clean cleanup. See [live trial](live-opencode-trial.md) and [machine-readable evidence](live-opencode-evidence.json). Earlier fixture counts and provider skips above describe their specific runs.
export async function completeIssues(
  store: RunStore,
  adapter: OpenCodeAdapter,
  input: z.infer<typeof completeIssuesInput>,
  sessionId: string,
) {
  const value = completeIssuesInput.parse(input);
  const key = `${stateKey(store)}:${value.runId}`;
  const previous = issueCompletions.get(key);
  const operation = (previous ?? Promise.resolve())
    .catch(() => {})
    .then(() => acceptIssues(store, adapter, value, sessionId));
  issueCompletions.set(key, operation);
  try {
    return await operation;
  } finally {
    if (issueCompletions.get(key) === operation) issueCompletions.delete(key);
  }
}

async function acceptIssues(
  store: RunStore,
  adapter: OpenCodeAdapter,
  value: z.infer<typeof completeIssuesInput>,
  sessionId: string,
) {
  let { run, state } = await assertPlanningOwner(
    store,
    value.runId,
    value.attempt,
    sessionId,
  );
  if (
    state.owner?.retired ||
    state.owner?.stage !== "issues" ||
    !state.prd ||
    !state.approval?.sha ||
    !state.explanation ||
    state.approval.hash !== explanationHash(state.explanation) ||
    state.approval.revision !== state.explanation.revision
  )
    throw new Error(
      "Current issue stage ownership and explanation authorization required",
    );
  if (state.pending || state.revisionProposal)
    return {
      outcome: "blocked",
      requestId: state.pending?.requestId,
      message: "Resolve the pending product decision or scope approval first",
    };
  if (state.issueAcceptance?.result) return state.issueAcceptance.result;
  try {
    const config = await loadProjectConfig(state.workspace);
    if (
      commandConfigHash(config.commands) !== state.approval.commandHash ||
      config.commands.revision !== state.approval.commandRevision ||
      (await readReviewGit(state.workspace, ["rev-parse", "HEAD"])).trim() !==
        state.approval.sha
    )
      throw new Error("Approved commit or command configuration changed");
  } catch (cause) {
    const next = structuredClone(state);
    next.error = {
      code: "baseline-blocked",
      message: `Restore approved inputs or renew explanation approval: ${String(cause)}`,
    };
    await savePlanning(store, run.runId, run.revision, next);
    return { outcome: "blocked", message: next.error.message };
  }
  try {
    const prdText = await readFile(
      await readPlanningArtifact(state.workspace, state.prd.artifactPath),
      "utf8",
    );
    if (artifactHash(prdText) !== state.prd.hash)
      throw new Error("Accepted PRD changed");
  } catch (cause) {
    const next = structuredClone(state);
    next.error = {
      code: "baseline-blocked",
      message: `Accepted PRD unavailable or changed: ${String(cause)}`,
    };
    await savePlanning(store, run.runId, run.revision, next);
    return { outcome: "blocked", message: next.error.message };
  }
  if (!state.issueAcceptance) {
    try {
      const draft = `.ralph/features/${run.runId}/issues-draft-${value.attempt}/`;
      const read = async (path: string) => {
        const text = await readFile(
          await readPlanningArtifact(state.workspace, draft + path),
          "utf8",
        );
        if (state.managedArtifacts?.[draft + path] !== artifactHash(text))
          throw new Error(`Missing or changed owned draft ${path}`);
        return text;
      };
      const raw = JSON.parse(await read(value.graphPath));
      const files: Record<string, string> = {};
      if (!Array.isArray(raw.issues)) throw new Error("Graph requires issues");
      for (const issue of raw.issues) {
        if (
          typeof issue.artifactPath !== "string" ||
          !/^issues\/[1-9][0-9]*-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*\.md$/.test(
            issue.artifactPath,
          )
        )
          throw new Error("Invalid issue draft reference");
        files[issue.artifactPath] = await read(issue.artifactPath);
      }
      const graph = validateIssueGraph(raw, files, state.prd);
      const next = structuredClone(state);
      const graphRevision =
        Math.max(
          0,
          ...(state.history ?? []).map((h) => h.graph?.revision ?? 0),
        ) + 1;
      next.issueAcceptance = {
        sessionId,
        attempt: value.attempt,
        graph: {
          revision: graphRevision,
          hash: issueBundleHash(graph, files),
          artifactPath: `.ralph/features/${run.runId}/graph-${graphRevision}/graph.json`,
          value: graph,
          files,
          order: topologicalOrder(graph),
        },
      };
      await savePlanning(store, run.runId, run.revision, next);
    } catch (cause) {
      // Store ownership/revision failures must not be reported as successful acceptance.
      if (String(cause).includes("Stale")) throw cause;
      return {
        outcome: "correctable-invalid",
        message: `Fix issue drafts and retry ralph_complete_issues: ${String(cause)}`,
      };
    }
  }
  ({ run, state } = await assertPlanningOwner(
    store,
    value.runId,
    value.attempt,
    sessionId,
  ));
  const intent = state.issueAcceptance;
  if (
    !intent ||
    !state.approval?.sha ||
    !state.approval.commandHash ||
    !state.approval.commandRevision ||
    !state.prd
  )
    throw new Error("Missing issue acceptance intent or scope authorization");
  if (!state.graph) {
    const next = structuredClone(state);
    const files = {
      ...intent.graph.files,
      "graph.json": JSON.stringify(intent.graph.value, null, 2),
    };
    const publication = await publishRevision(
      store,
      run.revision,
      next,
      run.runId,
      `graph-${intent.graph.revision}`,
      files,
      `complete-issues-${value.attempt}`,
    );
    next.graph = intent.graph;
    next.managedArtifacts = { ...next.managedArtifacts };
    for (const [path, text] of Object.entries(files))
      next.managedArtifacts[`${publication.directory}/${path}`] =
        artifactHash(text);
    next.selectedCommit = state.approval.sha;
    next.executionEligibility = {
      revision: intent.graph.revision,
      selectedCommit: state.approval.sha,
      prdHash: state.prd.hash,
      graphHash: intent.graph.hash,
      requestId: state.approval.requestId,
      messageId: state.approval.messageId,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      explanationHash: state.approval.hash,
      commandHash: state.approval.commandHash,
      commandRevision: state.approval.commandRevision,
    };
    next.phase = "execution_approved";
    delete next.error;
    await savePlanning(store, run.runId, publication.revision, next);
  }
  const latest = await store.getRun(run.runId);
  if (!latest?.planning?.issueAcceptance)
    throw new Error("Issue acceptance lost");
  const next = structuredClone(latest.planning);
  const result = {
    outcome: "accepted" as const,
    graph: {
      revision: intent.graph.revision,
      hash: intent.graph.hash,
      artifactPath: intent.graph.artifactPath,
    },
  };
  if (!next.issueAcceptance) throw new Error("Missing issue acceptance");
  next.issueAcceptance.result = result;
  await savePlanning(store, run.runId, latest.revision, next);
  // Durable acceptance precedes effects. Interrupted execution resumes through the
  // existing explicit recovery path; tool retries never redispatch accepted work.
  await continuePlanning(store, adapter, run.runId);
  return result;
}

